# The mouse wheel scrolls a thread, and the follow-latch gets out of its way.
#
# WHAT SHIPPED. The wheel moved a transcript exactly zero pixels. Not a dead
# event and not a missed hit-test — the notch arrived, moved `scroll_target`,
# and was erased by the pane's own bottom-pin on the very next frame. afterhours
# writes a notch to the TARGET (plugins/ui/systems.h HandleScrollInput) and eases
# the OFFSET toward it at 0.28 of the remaining distance per frame, so one frame
# after a notch the offset has moved 5.6 px; the follow-latch re-armed for
# anything within 24 px of the end, and re-pinned. The offset could never leave
# the band. See src/ecs/follow_latch.h for the full account.
#
# Against that build, this script fails three ways:
#
#   [TIMEOUT] assert_ui (line 8): Command 'assert_ui' timed out after 30 frames
#   [E2E ERROR] assert_ui (line 9): assert_ui 'transcript_bottom_pad':
#       y=766 but got 646
#   [E2E ERROR] expect_text (line 18): Text not found:
#       'Follow-up question #0:'
#
# — the jump-to-bottom affordance never appeared because the latch never let go,
# the transcript never moved, and 800 notches' worth of wheel never reached the
# top of the thread.
#
# COORDINATES, AND WHERE THEY CAME FROM. `mouse_move 650 400` is the middle of
# the transcript pane at this window size; the runner has no element-addressed
# pointer move (afterhours_gaps.md #285), so a gesture is spelled in absolute
# window coordinates. 646 is where `transcript_bottom_pad` sits when the pane is
# pinned to the end, measured with `assert_ui transcript_bottom_pad y=-1`; it is
# asserted at the top of the script as well as after the round trip, so a layout
# change fails as "the pinned position moved" rather than as "the wheel broke".
#
# 766 is 646 + 120, and the 120 is 3 notches x 20 px x TWO frames: the e2e
# injector deliberately lets a wheel delta survive one reset_frame, because its
# reader can run before the command that sets it, so one `scroll_wheel` command
# is read on two consecutive frames. That is the harness, not the app — a real
# notch is 60 px here. If that survival rule ever changes, this is the number
# that moves.
#
# HERMETIC: opens its own thread through HANABI_OPEN and starts from no tabs, so
# it neither inherits a thread from an earlier script nor leaves one behind.
#
# NOTE: expect_no_text takes a BARE single word — see afterhours_gaps.md #47.
#
# settings: {"window_width":1100,"window_height":760,"open_tabs":[],"active_tab":"","theme":"dark"}
# env: HANABI_BIG_TRANSCRIPT=1 HANABI_OPEN=rbig

# 1. A thread opens pinned to its newest message. require_thread states the
#    precondition in the script rather than leaving it to the `# env:` line, so
#    a run that inherited someone else's thread fails saying so.
require_thread rbig
mouse_move 650 400
wait_frames 10
assert_ui transcript_bottom_pad y=646

# 2. One notch of the wheel, with the pointer over the transcript, moves the
#    transcript. This one line is the whole bug report.
scroll_wheel 0 3
wait_frames 25
assert_ui jump_to_bottom hidden=false
assert_ui transcript_bottom_pad y=766

# 3. Scrolling up broke the follow-latch — which is what the jump-to-bottom
#    affordance above means, since it is drawn only when the pane is NOT
#    following. Keep wheeling and the top of the thread arrives.
scroll_wheel 0 200
wait_frames 20
scroll_wheel 0 200
wait_frames 20
scroll_wheel 0 200
wait_frames 20
scroll_wheel 0 200
wait_frames 30
expect_text "Follow-up question #0:"

# 4. Wheeling back down returns to the end...
scroll_wheel 0 -200
wait_frames 20
scroll_wheel 0 -200
wait_frames 20
scroll_wheel 0 -200
wait_frames 20
scroll_wheel 0 -200
wait_frames 30
expect_no_text #0:

# 5. ...and re-arms the latch, so the pane is pinned to the end again — exactly
#    where step 1 found it, not merely near it. A pane that had stopped
#    following would sit wherever the last notch left it.
assert_ui transcript_bottom_pad y=646
